        [
            'title' => 'What Phonological Awareness Is and Why It Matters',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Phonological awareness is the ability to notice and manipulate the sound structure of spoken language, independent of print. It sits on a continuum from large, easy units to small, difficult ones: first words within sentences, then syllables within words, then onset-rime (the initial sound versus the rest of the word, as in "c-at"), and finally individual phonemes. The National Reading Panel identified phonemic awareness, along with phonics, fluency, vocabulary, and comprehension, as one of the five pillars essential to reading instruction.</p><p>Children typically develop phonological awareness before they can read, and strength in this area in kindergarten is one of the strongest predictors of later decoding success. Students who struggle to hear that "cat" and "hat" rhyme, or who cannot clap out the two syllables in "ta-ble," are likely to struggle when asked to map letters to sounds. Because these skills are entirely oral, they can and should be practiced without any text in front of the child, through songs, clapping games, and listening activities woven into daily routines.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'Think of a student who struggles with phonics instruction. Could the underlying issue actually be a weaker oral phonological awareness skill rather than letter-sound knowledge? How would you check?']],
