        [
            'title' => 'Rhyme and Syllable Activities',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Rhyme and syllable awareness are the earliest, most accessible phonological skills and make an ideal entry point for whole-class instruction. Rhyme activities include reading predictable rhyming picture books and pausing before the rhyming word, playing "odd one out" with three words where two rhyme, and generating rhyming word families together as a class. Syllable awareness is built through clapping, stomping, or using counters to mark each syllable in a child\'s name, a classroom object, or a word from a story just read aloud.</p><p>These activities work best in short, high-frequency bursts of five to ten minutes rather than long isolated lessons, embedded into transitions, calendar time, or snack time. Teachers should model the skill first, then have the whole group respond together before calling on individuals, so students who are still developing the skill are not singled out before they are ready.</p>']],
