        [
            'title' => 'Phoneme Blending and Segmentation',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Phoneme-level skills are the most demanding form of phonological awareness and the ones most directly tied to decoding and spelling. Blending is hearing separate sounds, /m/ /a/ /p/, and fusing them into the word "map." Segmentation is the reverse: hearing "map" and pulling it apart into its three sounds. Both skills are typically taught with manipulatives, such as moving a chip into a box for each sound heard, before moving to purely oral, no-manipulative versions.</p><p>Phoneme manipulation, such as deleting the first sound in "stop" to get "top," or substituting the vowel in "sit" to make "sat," is the most advanced skill in this strand and is usually introduced only after blending and segmentation are secure. Teachers should keep phoneme-level tasks oral and brief, since the goal is auditory sound manipulation, not letter recognition; print is deliberately withheld at this stage so that struggling readers are not penalized for weak letter knowledge during a listening task.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'assignment', 'content' => ['instructions' => '<p>Design a five-minute phoneme blending and segmentation warm-up for a small group of kindergarten students, using counters or chips. Specify the exact words you would use, the order you would present them in (CV, VC, then CVC), and one way you would adapt the activity for a student who is not yet succeeding.</p>', 'files' => [], 'due_date' => null]],
