        [
            'title' => 'Screening and Classroom Integration',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Because phonological awareness is foundational, many schools use brief universal screeners two to three times a year in kindergarten and first grade to flag students who may need additional small-group practice. These screeners typically sample rhyme judgment, syllable segmentation, and phoneme blending or segmentation in a few minutes per child, and results are used to form flexible small groups rather than to label students permanently.</p><p>Day-to-day, phonological awareness integrates naturally into shared reading, morning meeting, and transition routines, so it does not require a separate block of the schedule. Teachers who weave in a quick sound game while lining up for lunch, or a syllable clap during attendance, give students the repeated, low-stakes practice that builds this skill fastest, while reserving targeted small-group instruction for students the screening data shows are furthest behind.</p>']],
