    'modules' => [
        [
            'title' => 'Recognizing Dyslexia in the Classroom',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Dyslexia is a specific, neurobiological learning difference characterized by difficulty with accurate and fluent word recognition, decoding, and spelling, despite otherwise typical cognitive ability and instruction. It is not a vision problem and is not caused by lack of motivation or intelligence; most researchers link it to differences in phonological processing, meaning the brain\'s ability to work with the sound structure of language.</p><p>Classroom signs vary by age but commonly include slow, effortful decoding of unfamiliar words, difficulty with phonics despite repeated instruction, weak spelling that does not match a student\'s oral vocabulary, avoidance of reading aloud, and fatigue after reading tasks. Many dyslexic students compensate by memorizing sight words or relying heavily on picture and context cues, which can mask the underlying decoding weakness until texts become more complex in later grades.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'A student in your class reads fluently for the first few pages of a leveled text but becomes noticeably slower and more frustrated as soon as unfamiliar multisyllabic words appear. What might this pattern suggest, and what would you want to observe next?']],
            ],
