        [
            'title' => 'Structured Literacy and Multisensory Instruction',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'research_reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Structured literacy is an umbrella term for explicit, systematic instruction in the components of language that underlie reading: phonology, sound-symbol association, syllable structure, morphology, syntax, and semantics. It is delivered cumulatively, with each new skill building on previously mastered ones, and diagnostically, with teachers continually checking whether a student has truly mastered a skill before moving on. The Orton-Gillingham approach, developed from the work of Samuel Orton and Anna Gillingham in the early twentieth century, is one of the most established structured literacy methods and heavily influences many commercial programs used today.</p><p>A defining feature of Orton-Gillingham-informed instruction is multisensory teaching, which engages visual, auditory, and kinesthetic-tactile pathways simultaneously, for example having a student see a letter, say its sound, and trace it in sand or on textured paper at the same time. This redundancy helps build stronger, more durable memory connections for students whose phonological processing makes single-pathway instruction less effective. While full Orton-Gillingham tutoring is usually delivered by a trained specialist in small groups, classroom teachers can borrow its core principles of explicit, sequential, multisensory practice for whole-class phonics instruction.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'graded_quiz', 'content' => ['questions' => [
                    ['question' => 'What is dyslexia primarily understood to be rooted in?', 'options' => [['text' => 'A vision tracking problem', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Differences in phonological processing', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Low general intelligence', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Lack of reading motivation', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Research consistently links dyslexia to differences in how the brain processes the sound structure of language (phonological processing), not vision or intelligence.'],
                    ['question' => 'What does "multisensory" mean in the context of Orton-Gillingham-informed instruction?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Using many different books', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Engaging visual, auditory, and kinesthetic-tactile pathways together', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Teaching in a sensory room', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Using only auditory instruction', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Multisensory instruction combines seeing, saying, and physically forming letters or sounds at once to reinforce memory through multiple pathways.'],
                    ['question' => 'Which best describes structured literacy instruction?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Random, whole-group exposure to texts', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Explicit, systematic, and cumulative teaching of language components', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Purely independent silent reading', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Teaching spelling and reading as entirely separate, unrelated subjects', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Structured literacy is explicit (directly taught, not inferred), systematic, and cumulative, building each skill on mastered prior skills.'],
                ], 'pass_percent' => 70]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Classroom Accommodations That Preserve Rigor',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Accommodations change how a dyslexic student accesses content, not what they are expected to learn, and should be paired with continued skill-building instruction rather than replacing it. Common, well-supported accommodations include extended time on reading and writing tasks, audiobooks or text-to-speech for grade-level content the student cannot yet decode independently, reduced copying demands, and permission to type or dictate longer written responses.</p><p>Teachers sometimes worry that accommodations lower expectations, but the opposite is usually true: removing the decoding bottleneck lets a dyslexic student demonstrate the same depth of thinking as peers on content, discussion, and analysis, while targeted phonics and spelling instruction continues separately to build the underlying skill. Accommodations should be revisited periodically rather than treated as permanent and unchanging, since a student\'s needs shift as decoding and fluency improve.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'assignment', 'content' => ['instructions' => '<p>Choose a specific grade-level assignment from your own curriculum (for example, a social studies reading or a writing task). Describe three accommodations you would offer a dyslexic student for that exact assignment, and explain how each preserves the academic expectation rather than lowering it.</p>', 'files' => [], 'due_date' => null]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Collaborating Across the School Team',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Supporting a dyslexic student well rarely falls on one teacher alone. Classroom teachers, reading specialists, special education staff, and families each hold different pieces of the picture, from formal evaluation data to day-to-day classroom observations, and consistent communication among them keeps instruction and accommodations aligned. When a student has a formal diagnosis and an individualized education program or similar support plan, the classroom teacher is typically responsible for implementing specified accommodations with fidelity and flagging when something is not working.</p><p>Equally important is how dyslexia is discussed with the student and the class. Framing dyslexia accurately, as a difference in how the brain processes language rather than a deficit in effort or intelligence, helps protect a student\'s confidence and willingness to keep trying difficult reading tasks in front of peers.</p>']],
