    'modules' => [
        [
            'title' => 'Comprehensible Input in Practice',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Comprehensible input, a concept most associated with linguist Stephen Krashen, describes language that a learner can understand even though it contains some structures or vocabulary slightly beyond their current level. The goal is not simplified, "baby talk" language, but input made accessible through context: gestures, visuals, realia, slower and clearer speech, repetition of key phrases, and previewing vocabulary before a lesson begins.</p><p>In a mainstream classroom, this might look like a teacher pairing every key academic term with a picture or quick demonstration, chunking directions into short steps with a visual checklist, and consistently using the same sentence frames across subjects so English language learners are not decoding new sentence structures every time a new skill is introduced. Content expectations stay the same; what changes is how the content is delivered so the student can access it.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'Think of a recent lesson you taught. Which parts of your spoken instructions might have been difficult for a student with limited English proficiency to follow, independent of the content itself? What would you adjust?']],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Scaffolding by Proficiency Level',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Many schools use a proficiency framework similar to WIDA\'s levels, ranging from entering and emerging at the early stages to bridging and reaching at the advanced stages, to describe a student\'s current English language development across listening, speaking, reading, and writing. These levels are a starting point for planning, not a ceiling on what a student can think about or achieve.</p><p>Scaffolding should match the proficiency level: an entering-level student might respond to a comprehension question by pointing to a picture or matching a word to an image, while a student at a bridging level might answer in a full sentence using a provided frame, and a reaching-level student might answer independently with light vocabulary support. The academic task and its rigor stay constant across the class; only the language demand of how the student demonstrates understanding changes.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'practical_quiz', 'content' => ['questions' => [
                    ['question' => 'What is the main idea behind comprehensible input?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Simplifying content so it requires no effort to understand', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Making language accessible through context while keeping content rigorous', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Only speaking in the student\'s home language', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Avoiding any new vocabulary', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Comprehensible input uses context, visuals, and clear delivery to make slightly challenging language accessible, without lowering academic content.'],
                    ['question' => 'A student at an early ("entering") English proficiency level is best supported by which response option for a comprehension check?', 'options' => [['text' => 'A full written paragraph with no support', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Pointing to or matching a picture', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'An independent oral presentation', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'A timed essay', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Entering-level students need low-language-demand ways to show understanding, such as pointing or matching, while still engaging with the actual content.'],
                    ['question' => 'What should stay consistent across proficiency levels when scaffolding a lesson?', 'options' => [['text' => 'The language demand', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'The academic rigor of the task', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'The amount of visual support given', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'The sentence frames provided', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Scaffolding changes the language demand and support provided, but the underlying academic expectation and rigor of the task should remain the same for all students.'],
                ]]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Translanguaging and Home Language Strengths',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'research_reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Translanguaging refers to the natural practice of multilingual speakers drawing on their full linguistic repertoire, moving fluidly between languages to make meaning, rather than keeping languages in strictly separate compartments. Research in bilingual education increasingly treats this as a resource to leverage rather than a problem to eliminate, since a student\'s home language often carries conceptual knowledge, vocabulary, and literacy skills that transfer to English.</p><p>Practical translanguaging strategies include allowing students to brainstorm or discuss ideas in their home language before writing in English, providing bilingual glossaries for content-specific vocabulary, and inviting students to make connections between cognates across their home language and English, such as "información" and "information" in Spanish and English. These approaches validate the home language as an asset while still working toward the goal of English academic proficiency.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'research_activity', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'Identify one student in your class (or a hypothetical composite profile) with a home language other than English. Research three to five cognates or related vocabulary connections between that language and English that would support an upcoming content unit, and note how you would introduce them.', 'requires_submission' => true]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Building an Inclusive Classroom Culture',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Beyond specific instructional techniques, the overall classroom culture strongly affects how comfortable English language learners feel taking academic risks. Simple structural choices, such as giving processing time before calling on students, allowing think-pair-share with a partner before whole-class discussion, and normalizing accents and occasional grammar errors as a natural part of language learning, reduce anxiety that can otherwise shut down participation entirely.</p><p>Family engagement matters as well. Sending key information home in a family\'s preferred language when possible, and inviting families to share cultural and linguistic knowledge relevant to classroom content, both strengthens the home-school connection and signals that the family\'s language and culture are valued assets in their child\'s education, not barriers to be overcome.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'case_study', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Mr. Osei has a new student, Lin, who arrived mid-year at an "emerging" English proficiency level. Lin is clearly strong in math, often solving problems correctly on scratch paper, but rarely volunteers answers and has stopped attempting written explanations since a classmate laughed at a spelling mistake three weeks ago. Mr. Osei wants to rebuild Lin\'s confidence while still pushing her English development, and has a Mandarin-speaking paraprofessional available two periods a week.</p>', 'links' => [['url' => 'https://alzahra.institute/resources', 'reference' => 'Al Zahra Institute Resource Library']]]],
