[
    'title' => 'Inclusive Teaching for Autism Spectrum Communication Needs',
    'audience' => 'teachers',
    'level' => 'intermediate',
    'short_desc' => 'Strategies for supporting the communication, social, and sensory needs of autistic students in mainstream classrooms, including visual schedules, social stories, and AAC awareness.',
    'outcomes' => [
        'Implement visual schedules and predictable routines that reduce anxiety and support transitions',
        'Use social narratives appropriately to teach social expectations without scripting forced social behavior',
        'Recognize when a student may benefit from augmentative and alternative communication and collaborate with specialists',
    ],
    'modules' => [
        [
            'title' => 'Understanding Autism and Communication Diversity',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Autism spectrum differences affect social communication and often involve restricted or repetitive patterns of behavior, interests, or sensory responses, but the presentation varies enormously from one student to another. Some autistic students are highly verbal and struggle primarily with the unwritten rules of social interaction, such as reading tone or knowing when a conversation has shifted topics; others are minimally speaking or non-speaking and rely partly or fully on alternative communication methods.</p><p>A central shift in how educators think about autism is moving away from viewing these differences purely as deficits to compensate for, and toward recognizing autistic communication styles, such as scripting, echolalia, or intense focus on specific interests, as valid ways of processing and engaging with the world. Effective inclusive teaching starts from genuine curiosity about how a specific student communicates and what support actually helps them, rather than a one-size-fits-all checklist.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'A colleague describes a student\'s repeated phrases from a favorite video (echolalia) as "just noise" that should be redirected away. How would you reframe this behavior, and what might it actually be communicating?']],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Visual Schedules and Predictable Structure',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Many autistic students experience significant anxiety around uncertainty and unexpected transitions, since the classroom day is full of implicit changes that neurotypical students often navigate without conscious effort. Visual schedules, a sequence of pictures, icons, or words showing the order of the day\'s activities, make these transitions explicit and predictable, reducing anxiety and the behavioral responses that anxiety can trigger.</p><p>Effective visual schedules are referenced consistently, not just posted and ignored, with the teacher or student physically checking off or moving a marker for each completed activity. Advance warning of changes to the usual routine, such as a substitute teacher or an assembly, delivered visually and in advance whenever possible, further reduces the disruption that unexpected change can cause. These supports benefit the whole class but are often essential, rather than optional, for autistic students.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'assignment', 'content' => ['instructions' => '<p>Create a simple visual schedule template for a half-day of your actual classroom routine, using icons, words, or both. Include at least one built-in way to signal an upcoming change to the normal routine (such as an early dismissal or assembly).</p>', 'files' => [], 'due_date' => null]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Social Narratives and Sensory Considerations',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Social narratives, sometimes called social stories, are short, individualized texts that describe a specific social situation, the relevant social cues, and expected or possible responses, written in a calm, descriptive, non-judgmental tone. They are used to preview unfamiliar or anxiety-provoking situations, such as a fire drill or a field trip, not to force a student into performing neurotypical social behavior that doesn\'t fit them. A well-written social narrative describes what will happen and offers options, rather than issuing commands.</p><p>Sensory sensitivities are common among autistic students and can include heightened or reduced sensitivity to sound, light, touch, or movement. Classroom accommodations might include allowing noise-reducing headphones during loud activities, offering a quiet corner or break space, permitting movement or fidget tools during seated work, and giving advance notice before fire drills or other loud, unpredictable events whenever possible.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'graded_quiz', 'content' => ['questions' => [
                    ['question' => 'What is the primary purpose of a social narrative (social story)?', 'options' => [['text' => 'To command a student to behave a certain way', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'To preview a social situation calmly and describe expected cues and responses', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'To punish unwanted behavior', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'To replace all verbal communication', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Social narratives are descriptive previews of situations meant to reduce anxiety and build understanding, not commands or behavior scripts.'],
                    ['question' => 'Why are visual schedules particularly helpful for many autistic students?', 'options' => [['text' => 'They make the day more colorful', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'They make the sequence of events explicit and predictable, reducing anxiety', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'They are required by law in every classroom', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'They replace the need for any teacher instruction', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Visual schedules reduce the uncertainty around transitions and routine changes, which is often a major source of anxiety for autistic students.'],
                    ['question' => 'Which is an appropriate sensory accommodation for a student sensitive to loud noise?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Requiring the student to sit near the loudspeaker', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Allowing noise-reducing headphones during loud activities', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Removing the student from all group activities permanently', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Ignoring the sensitivity since it will pass', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Noise-reducing headphones are a simple, respectful accommodation that lets the student remain included while managing sensory discomfort.'],
                ], 'pass_percent' => 70]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'AAC Awareness and Team Collaboration',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Augmentative and alternative communication, or AAC, refers to any tool or method that supplements or replaces spoken speech, ranging from low-tech picture exchange boards to dedicated speech-generating devices and tablet-based AAC apps. A student using AAC is communicating just as meaningfully as a speaking student, and classroom teachers play an important role in making sure the device or system is available, charged, and actually used throughout the school day, not just during speech therapy sessions.</p><p>Classroom teachers do not need to become AAC experts, but should learn the basics of a specific student\'s system from the speech-language pathologist or AAC specialist on the team: how to model language on it, how to give the student time to formulate a response, and how to include AAC-based responses as valid participation in class discussion. Presuming competence, assuming the student has something meaningful to say and giving them the tools and time to say it, is a foundational principle across all AAC use.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'case_study', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>A non-speaking fourth grader uses a tablet-based AAC app to communicate and has made strong progress in speech therapy sessions, building longer sentences there. In the classroom, however, his teacher often answers for him when he pauses to navigate his AAC screen, and other students have started doing the same during group work. The student has begun participating less during class discussions even though his AAC skills are clearly growing in therapy. The speech-language pathologist wants to find a way to help the classroom teacher and peers give him the processing time his AAC use requires.</p>', 'links' => [['url' => 'https://alzahra.institute/resources', 'reference' => 'Al Zahra Institute Resource Library']]]],
            ],
        ],
    ],
],
