        [
            'title' => 'The Link Between Oral Language and Reading',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Reading comprehension depends on far more than decoding; it depends on understanding the language being decoded. This relationship is often described through the "simple view of reading," which frames reading comprehension as the product of decoding skill and language comprehension. A student can decode a sentence perfectly and still fail to understand it if their oral vocabulary and grammatical knowledge of English have not developed enough to support the content.</p><p>Oral language weaknesses often go unnoticed in early grades because decoding challenges tend to be addressed first and are easier to measure with simple assessments. However, a student with thin vocabulary or weak sentence-level language skills frequently hits a wall in third or fourth grade, when texts shift from simple, familiar sentence structures to more complex academic language. Building oral language proactively in earlier grades, through rich classroom talk, read-alouds, and structured discussion, helps prevent this later comprehension drop.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'A student decodes fluently and accurately but frequently cannot answer basic comprehension questions about what they just read aloud. What might this suggest about where to focus instruction, beyond more decoding practice?']],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Building Vocabulary Depth Through Talk',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Vocabulary instruction is most effective when it builds depth of word knowledge, not just a surface-level definition. A student who can define "enormous" as "very big" but has never used the word in a sentence or heard it in varied contexts has only shallow knowledge of it. Rich vocabulary instruction involves introducing a word during a read-aloud, providing a student-friendly explanation, offering examples and non-examples, and then creating repeated opportunities across the week to use the word orally in discussion, retelling, or partner talk.</p><p>Classroom talk structures such as partner turn-and-talk, small-group discussion with sentence starters, and teacher-led "think aloud" modeling all give students repeated low-stakes practice using new vocabulary and more complex sentence structures out loud before they are expected to use that same language independently in writing. The goal is for oral language to serve as a rehearsal space for the language students will later need on the page.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'practical_quiz', 'content' => ['questions' => [
                    ['question' => 'According to the "simple view of reading," reading comprehension is the product of which two components?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Decoding and handwriting speed', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Decoding and language comprehension', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Vocabulary and spelling only', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Fluency and motivation', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'The simple view of reading frames comprehension as the product of decoding skill and language (listening) comprehension; weakness in either limits overall comprehension.'],
                    ['question' => 'What distinguishes "deep" vocabulary knowledge from shallow knowledge of a word?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Only being able to spell the word correctly', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Being able to use the word flexibly across varied contexts, not just recite a definition', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Recognizing the word\'s first letter quickly', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Knowing how many syllables it has', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Deep vocabulary knowledge means understanding a word well enough to use and recognize it flexibly across different contexts, not just recalling a single definition.'],
                    ['question' => 'Why might a student\'s oral language weaknesses go unnoticed in early grades?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Because oral language does not matter for reading', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Because early decoding challenges are often addressed first and are easier to measure', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Because all students have identical oral language skills by kindergarten', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Because oral language is only assessed in high school', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Decoding problems are easier to spot with simple assessments early on, so oral language and vocabulary gaps can remain hidden until texts become more linguistically complex.'],
                ]]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Syntax and Sentence-Level Language',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Beyond vocabulary, students need oral practice with the grammatical structures that appear in written academic text, such as complex sentences using subordinating conjunctions ("although," "because," "unless") and embedded clauses. Many students speak in simple, short sentences in casual conversation but rarely encounter or produce the longer, more complex sentence structures typical of academic writing unless a teacher deliberately models and practices them aloud.</p><p>Sentence-combining and sentence-expansion activities, where students take two short oral sentences and combine them using a connecting word, or expand a simple sentence by adding "when," "where," or "why" details, build exactly this kind of syntactic flexibility. Practicing these structures out loud before writing, through choral repetition, sentence imitation games, and oral sentence-building with picture prompts, reduces the cognitive load of having to invent both the idea and the complex grammar simultaneously when a student later writes.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'assignment', 'content' => ['instructions' => '<p>Design a ten-minute oral sentence-expansion activity using a picture or short video clip as a prompt. Include at least three sentence-starter frames you would use to help students produce longer, more complex oral sentences, and explain how you would scaffold a student who responds only in short phrases.</p>', 'files' => [], 'due_date' => null]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Narrative Structure and Retelling',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Understanding narrative structure, characters, setting, problem, sequence of events, and resolution, is a skill that transfers directly to reading comprehension, since most early texts are organized narratively. Structured oral retelling, where a student recounts a story just heard or read using a visual story map or sequencing prompts ("first," "next," "then," "finally"), builds this structural understanding while also reinforcing vocabulary and sentence-level language from the earlier parts of this module.</p><p>Teachers can scaffold retelling with props, puppets, or picture cards for students who need visual support, gradually removing these supports as a student\'s independent retelling becomes more complete and sequential. Comparing a student\'s retelling to the key story elements it is missing, rather than grading it as simply right or wrong, gives teachers a clear, specific target for the next round of instruction and practice.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'research_paper', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>A first-grade teacher notices that several students can answer literal recall questions about a story ("What did the character find?") but cannot retell the story in sequence or explain why the character made a particular choice. Their retellings include isolated events but lack causal connections and a clear structure. The teacher wants to determine whether this is a narrative-structure gap, a vocabulary gap, or a working-memory load issue, since the three would call for different instructional responses, and plans to compare retellings with and without a visual story map to help isolate the cause.</p>', 'links' => [['url' => 'https://alzahra.institute/resources', 'reference' => 'Al Zahra Institute Resource Library']]]],
